Feel the bounce: rumble the pad where the bounce sound is mixed.

The same change Neverball carries as its patch 0007, in the other binary of the same
tree. `sol_step` returns the bounce strength, upstream turns it into the volume the bump
is mixed at, and this passes the same number to the pad - so the speaker and the motors
agree by construction rather than by two separate intensity calculations kept in step.

The patch is one call and one include because everything hard about rumble is on the
other side of it. `oops_input_set_rumble` sets a *state*, so a bump needs something to
turn it off afterwards; that is the decay thread in `common/haptics.c`, shared by every
port, started and stopped by the shim around `main`.

diff --git a/putt/game.c b/putt/game.c
index 2cd6e1c..ee8292b 100644
--- a/putt/game.c
+++ b/putt/game.c
@@ -32,6 +32,10 @@
 #include "solid_sim.h"
 #include "solid_all.h"
 
+/* Pad rumble on a bounce - the port's, shared with every other title here rather than written
+   per game. `common/` is on the include path for this build. */
+#include "haptics.h"
+
 /*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
 
 static struct s_full file;
@@ -554,7 +558,17 @@ int game_step(const float g[3], float dt)
         /* Mix the sound of a ball bounce. */
 
         if (b > 0.5f)
-            audio_play(AUD_BUMP, (b - 0.5f) * 2.0f);
+        {
+            const float k = (b - 0.5f) * 2.0f;
+
+            audio_play(AUD_BUMP, k);
+
+            /* **The same event, felt as well as heard.** `k` is the strength the sound is
+             * already mixed at, so the pad and the speaker agree by construction. Turning the
+             * motors off again belongs to `common/haptics.h`, whose decay thread the shim
+             * starts - see Neverball's patch 0007, which is this change in the other binary. */
+            oops_haptics_bump(k);
+        }
     }
 
     game_update_view(dt);
